1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:07.119 line:2 align:center
A synthetic test set of 200
patients. Model A is right on 170,

2
00:00:07.119 --> 00:00:11.129 line:2 align:center
85%, and model B on 164,

3
00:00:11.129 --> 00:00:15.750 line:2 align:center
82%. The question is how much
of that three-point gap is just

4
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:19.283 line:2 align:center
which patients we happened to test.

5
00:00:19.483 --> 00:00:24.988 line:2 align:center
The bootstrap answers that by resampling.
Draw 200 patients with replacement,

6
00:00:24.988 --> 00:00:31.327 line:2 align:center
so some appear more than once, recompute
the accuracy, and repeat 10,000 times.

7
00:00:31.327 --> 00:00:36.417 line:2 align:center
The spread of those 10,000
numbers is the uncertainty.

8
00:00:36.617 --> 00:00:42.098 line:2 align:center
Model A's interval runs from 0.80 to 0.90,

9
00:00:42.098 --> 00:00:47.278 line:2 align:center
and model B's from 0.77 to 0.87.

10
00:00:47.278 --> 00:00:51.907 line:2 align:center
They overlap, but overlapping
intervals are not a test.

11
00:00:51.907 --> 00:00:56.750 line:2 align:center
Each one answers a question
about one model on its own.

12
00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:01.005 line:2 align:center
Both models were graded
on the same 200 patients.

13
00:01:01.005 --> 00:01:06.628 line:2 align:center
A is right where B is wrong 14 times,
and B is right where A is wrong 8 times.

14
00:01:06.628 --> 00:01:13.283 line:2 align:center
14 minus 8 is 6 patients, which is
exactly the three-point difference.

15
00:01:13.483 --> 00:01:19.297 line:2 align:center
Bootstrapping the difference on the
same resampled patients gives 0.03,

16
00:01:19.297 --> 00:01:26.406 line:2 align:center
with a 95% interval from
minus 0.015 to 0.075.

17
00:01:26.406 --> 00:01:30.805 line:2 align:center
It includes zero, so these data
do not show that A is better.

18
00:01:30.805 --> 00:01:34.283 line:2 align:center
They do not show the
models are equal either.

19
00:01:34.483 --> 00:01:37.086 line:2 align:center
Pairing matters. On the same data,

20
00:01:37.086 --> 00:01:43.824 line:2 align:center
ignoring it widens the interval
to minus 0.045 to 0.105,

21
00:01:43.824 --> 00:01:49.050 line:2 align:center
about two thirds wider. Report
intervals rather than point estimates,

22
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.913 line:2 align:center
and compare models on the same patients.
